#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068455 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "a316177e-e646-576c-bcab-d6cb0a450a03" :question/math-academy-id "e-300" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}\\dfrac{x^3-27}{x-3}$." :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=3$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}  \\dfrac {x^3-27}{x-3} \n&= \\lim_{x\\to3}  \\dfrac {[x^3-27]'}{[x-3]'}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 3}  \\dfrac {3x^2}{1}\\[5pt]\n&=  3(3)^2\\[5pt]\n&= 27\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068456 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "95c88e72-86e2-5627-b121-beafc7986d76" :question/math-academy-id "e-301" :question/problem "Evaluate  $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\, \\frac {\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac {\\left[\\sin^2 (2x)\\right]'}{[x^2]'}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{2\\cdot \\sin (2x) \\cdot\\cos (2x)\\cdot 2}{2x}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}{2\\cos (2x)} \\cdot \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\\[5pt]\n&= 2\\cos(2\\cdot 0)\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\\[5pt]\n&= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\n}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0}.$ But that's okay, because we can just apply L'Hopital's rule again! We get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\\[5pt]\n&=  2\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac {[\\sin (2x)]'}{[x]'}\\[5pt]\n&=  2\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac {2\\cos (2x)}{1}\\[5pt]\n&= 4\\cos(2\\cdot 0)\\\n&=4.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068457 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "b9a1c3de-68cb-5e07-a71b-41f9ac77c832" :question/math-academy-id "e-302" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)} .$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are\n$\\displaystyle\n \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\ln\\left(x^2\\right) = -\\infty$ and $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2}{x^2} = \\infty$ respectively,\nso we have the case $\\dfrac{-\\infty}{\\infty}$.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{[\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)]'}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)'}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\left(1/x^2\\right)\\cdot 2x}{-\\left({4}/{x^3}\\right)}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{x^{-2} \\cdot 2x}{-4x^{-3} }\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{2x^{-1}}{-4x^{-3} }\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{1}{2}  \\lim_{x\\to 0} {x^2}\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\[5pt]\n&= 0.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068458 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "8e96079a-3933-5562-a4f5-ddd87ac5d05f" :question/math-academy-id "e-303" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}  \\frac {x^6}{5x^6-4} }.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty},$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {x^6}{5x^6-4} &= \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}  \\dfrac {[x^6]'}{[5x^6-4]'}\\\n&= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {6x^5}{5(6x^5)-0}\\\n&=  \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {1}{5}\\\n&= \\dfrac{1}{5}.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4617dc05-b7c3-54a3-8ba8-402846b15bc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296240} {:answer/id #uuid "fa083fa1-257f-52c1-8bdd-4e16083cfdf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296241} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5df6a8-9107-5e7c-a593-62f2641b4f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296242} {:answer/id #uuid "a4fa7d81-a547-5b0b-86d2-58082d6e4845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296243} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb5d759-46ce-5e4f-9224-43fce07f63e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cb5d759-46ce-5e4f-9224-43fce07f63e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296244} :answer-field/id #uuid "11cbad53-4033-52c7-8499-d026641bafc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c54e2735-245d-5798-9c48-ef40ec1547e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-31253" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{[{x}^{2}+x-6]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-4]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2x+1}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2)+1}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54c44539-981e-5a81-92d0-f00213c9265c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296276} {:answer/id #uuid "4919d0de-8116-5b72-91dd-335bf99cd522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c44539-981e-5a81-92d0-f00213c9265c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296276} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0cacba6-2b91-5aed-b8c1-d516321b9e10" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb212d02-e01a-5a05-96a9-bd2df6014b4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-336971" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{3}]^{′}}{[{ln}^{2}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[3{x}^{3}]^{′}}{{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
